### Auth config (if applicable)

```typescript
The complete configuration and `SecondaryStorage` implementation are included in the reproduction above.

Important details:

- Redis is the only session store.
- MongoDB `auth_sessions` remains empty throughout the reproduction.
- Cookie caching is disabled.
- No custom authentication hooks or application patches are used.
- `increment` and `getAndDelete` use atomic Redis operations.

Mongo adapter transactions and endpoint rate limiting are disabled **only in this minimal reproduction** to isolate secondary-storage session behavior. The original application also reproduced these defects with Mongo transactions and database-backed rate limiting enabled.
```

### Additional context

From reviewing the [v1.7.2 internal adapter](https://github.com/better-auth/better-auth/blob/v1.7.2/packages/better-auth/src/db/internal-adapter.ts), the relevant paths appear to be:

- Session creation performs a non-atomic read/modify/write of the serialized active-session list.
- `listSessions` and bulk revocation rely on that list, while `findSession` can accept a session-token record without corresponding index membership.
- Session updates read an existing record and later write it without an atomic condition preventing recreation after deletion.
- Session records and their index are updated separately during creation, refresh, and revocation.

The public revocation endpoints are implemented in [the session routes](https://github.com/better-auth/better-auth/blob/v1.7.2/packages/better-auth/src/api/routes/session.ts).

This concerns Better Auth's ordinary session cookies, not OAuth refresh-token rotation, cookie-cache expiration, or BSON ESM/CJS ObjectId normalization.

The original application uses Nuxt 4.5.2 and Nitro 2.13.4, but neither is involved in this standalone reproduction. I verified it on Windows using the versions above; other operating systems and database adapters have not been tested.

Could the secondary-storage session paths provide consistent indexing and revocation across concurrent creation, refresh, and deletion, including a fail-closed policy when index state is missing?

