### Additional context

- Reproducible with `curl` and Postman, not specific to any browser.
- Reproduces both locally (`next start` behind nginx with the default 60s read timeout) and in production. On a hosting platform without an upstream timeout (e.g. plain `next start` hit directly), the request hangs much longer than 60s instead of getting cut off — the underlying stall is the same, only the visible cap differs.
- The root cause appears to be the Server Component constraint that cookies cannot be written after streaming starts (see [Next.js cookies docs](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/api-reference/functions/cookies) and [vercel/next.js#49843](https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/49843)). Both writes that `getSession` performs in this code path — cookie cache refresh and rolling session refresh — fail in a way that leaves the metadata Suspense boundary unresolved, so the stream never closes.
- Likely related, possibly the same underlying issue:
  - #7008 — `auth.api.getSession()` returns null in Next.js App Router server components with cookie cache enabled (open)
  - #6799 — Session refresh writes on GET request (closed; this is where `disableSessionRefresh` was introduced)
  - #4188 — `getSession` returns null in Next.js server components
  - #5584 — `getServerSession` not working with `use cache` in Next.js 16
- The workaround at the end of the previous section is a clean fix for this app's symptoms, but it costs cookie cache and rolling refresh — both real features. A proper fix would let the Server Component path remain read-only without forcing users to give those up globally.

